{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 80,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our solar system is a tiny bubble of habitability \nsuspended in a vast universe that mostly wants to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 6400,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "kill us. In fact, a good fraction of our own \ngalaxy turns out to be utterly uninhabitable,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 12080,
    "dDurationMs": 7030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even for sun—like stellar systems. So is this why .. most of us .. haven’t seen aliens?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 24160,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our planet has a number of remarkable qualities \nthat seem to make it especially suitable for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 28560,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "developing and sustaining life, as we discussed \nin our episode on the rare earth hypothesis.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 33520,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But if you want life, one of the \nonly non-negotiable requirements  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 37680,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "has nothing to do with the planet. Our Sun \nalso seems pretty ideal in a number of ways.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 44160,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A bit more massive and it would have burned out \ntoo quickly, much less massive and it would be  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 48480,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "prone to erratic outbursts and wouldn’t produce \nenough ultraviolet light for photosynthesis.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 53840,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If the sun contained significantly less heavy elements it could never have formed a planetary system, but  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 60560,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "too much heavy elements and it might host only gas giants. Yep, the Sun seems pretty great."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 67040,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But is the Sun unique enough to explain one of \nthe most perplexing mysteries of the universe? In  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 72320,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a galaxy of 200+ billion stars, why don’t we see \nany other signs of technological life? This is the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 78880,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Fermi Paradox, which of course we’ve talked about \nbefore. But one possible solution is that the Sun  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 84240,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and its planetary system are really quite unique \nin its ability to spawn and nurture complex life.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 90960,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We tend to think of our home star as being \npretty important, but how unusual is it really?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 96800,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To help answer that question, I’d like to \nintroduce Dr. Moiya McTier - although perhaps  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 101680,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you already know her from her PBS show Fate \nand Fabled. What you probably don’t know is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 106560,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that Dr. McTier is a PhD astrophysicist, and expert in what we call the galactic habitability  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 113600,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "zone, as well as being an expert in folklore. \nWho better to compare the Sun’s uniqueness  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 119760,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from the perspectives of science versus \nour own slightly biased point of view."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 126000,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "From a modern astronomer’s perspective, \nour Sun is objectively mediocre,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 130560,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "average in terms of mass, age, even \nlocation. But consider for a moment  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 135760,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the ancient human’s perspective, without the \nhelp or hindrance of 21st century physics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 140400,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To our distant ancestors, the Sun \nwas a wondrous source of warmth,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 144320,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "light, food, and stability. It was a powerful, \nnever-tiring giver of life, and in mythology,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 150400,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "matters of life and death often get the most \nattention. This is why you see so many gods across  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 156000,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cultures with dominion over water, food, love, and \nwar, but nothing is more important to our survival  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 163440,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than the sun. So it’s no surprise \nthat nearly every ancient culture  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 167280,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "around the world worshipped a deity \nwho represented or personified the Sun."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 172480,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Many of these cultures cast their solar deities \nin myths that explained real natural phenomena,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 177600,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like the day/night cycle, eclipses, or the \nchanging of the seasons. To the Maori people  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 182800,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of New Zealand, the Sun was personified \nby the God Tamanuitera. In one story,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 188400,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Tamanuitera travels too quickly around the \nEarth, and humans complain that the days are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 193440,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "too short. His foster son, the hero Maui, \ntrapped Tamanuitera with a rope and didn't  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 199680,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "let him go until he promised to move more \nslowly across the sky. Little did the Maori  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 205440,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "know that Earth's days ARE getting longer, \nbut it's because of the moon, not the sun."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 209840,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This myth paints and others about the sun paint a nice picture of its importance."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 214480,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But pf course, modern science has \nits own origin story for our nearest and dearest star, the sun."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 220640,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Sun formed around 5 billion years ago, collapsing from an overdense lump of gas  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 225760,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "inside a much larger nebula, probably in one of \nthe great spiral arms of the Milky Way’s disk.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 231520,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "While most of the cloud ended up in the shrinking \nspheroid that would ultimately become our Sun,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 236560,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a fraction of the material collapsed \ninto a disk around the protostar.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 241040,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Heavier elements clumped together and slowly grew into planets - the smaller ones became terrestrial  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 246400,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "planets like the Earth, while the larger collected \nvast atmospheres of hydrogen and helium and became  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 252080,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the gas giants. At the same time, the core \nof the collapsing protostar became hotter  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 257280,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and hotter and eventually ignited in nuclear \nfusion. The outward flow of fusion-generated  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 263040,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energy supports the Sun against gravitational \ncollapse. It’s been resisting its own inward  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 268000,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "crush for 5 billion years now, and has \nenough fuel for about 5 billion more."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 272800,
    "dDurationMs": 8000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So our home star has a pretty cool backstory, but \nhardly a unique one. Our Sun is a pretty ordinary  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 280800,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "G-type main sequence star. Around 5% of the stars \nin the Milky Way are G-types, which means there  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 286720,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are several tens of billions of them. Having a \nplanetary system is also not unusual. The Kepler  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 292560,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mission demonstrated that most stars have planets \n- at least in the local part of the galaxy.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 298000,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Kepler also revealed that there are around 40 \nbillion Earth-analog planets in the Galaxy,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 304000,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that means rocky or terrestrial planets in the so-called habitable zone. That’s the distance from  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 310160,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the star where the intensity of light is in the \nright range to allow liquid water on the planet’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 315760,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "surface. So it sounds like the galaxy should \nbe full of potential starting points for life,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 320400,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even if we assume that life can \nonly form on Earth-like planets."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 324880,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But not so fast. There is another factor to consider.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 329280,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Stars have habitable zones, but so do galaxies. \nThere are huge regions of the Milky Way where  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 335600,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "life couldn’t possibly have formed, no \nmatter how perfect the host star. And  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 340720,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gues what - Moiya actually wrote her PhD thesis \non the galactic habitable zone. Moiya,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 347200,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "what are some of the factors that make \na region of the galaxy habitable or not?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 352720,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Stars are mostly made out of hydrogen \nand helium, with trace amounts of heavier  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 357120,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "elements. But those elements are critical - \nthey’re what planets like the Earth are made  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 361680,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out of. So firstly a star needs to form \nfrom a nebula at least some metallicity.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 367280,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "“Metallicity” is our measure of the \nheavy element content of a cloud  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 371040,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or a star. It comes from the fact that astronomers \ntend to call any element heavier than helium  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 376400,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a metal. On the other hand, if metallicity is \ntoo high, it can lead to the formation of too  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 382080,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "many gas giants like Jupiter - and that can also \ncause trouble for smaller Earth-sized planets."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 388160,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Most of these heavy elements are produced in \nmassive stars and then spread through the galaxy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 392720,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in supernova explosions. So a big factor \nin determining the galactic habitable zone  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 397920,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is that enough massive stars have \nlived and died in that region.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 402240,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But while we’re talking supernovae - \nit’s generally a bad idea to have too  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 406480,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "many exploding stars next door when you’re \ntrying to nurture a delicate biosphere. And  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 411120,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "some parts of the Milky Way were wracked by \nsupernovae for much of the Galaxy’s history."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 416400,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So it seems that to really understand the \nSun’s uniqueness, we need to take our story  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 420640,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "back even further - beyond the formation \nof the Sun to the formation of the Galaxy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 425920,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to figure out where habitable planetary systems could have formed in the first place. Our galaxies started  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 432480,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like all galaxies - as a slightly overdense spot \nin the near perfectly smooth cloud of particles  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 438800,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that filled the universe after the Big Bang. As \nit cooled, our local lump started to pull itself  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 444800,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "together under its own gravity. Fragments broke \noff to collapse into galaxies. Fragments within  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 450480,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "those fragments collapsed further, kindling little \nsparks of fusion reminiscent of an earlier epoch."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 456000,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This primordial generation of stars \nwere unpolluted by heavy elements,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 461360,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which means they couldn’t possibly have formed \nplanets. No chance for life yet. However these  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 466560,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stars were incredible atom factories, rapidly \nburning their way up the periodic table  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 472320,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and spraying heavy elements into the surrounding \ngas in colossal supernova explosions.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 477680,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The metallicity of the universe began to rise."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 481200,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The next generation of stars had some metals, and so for the first time had the chance to build  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 486240,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "planets. These stars fell towards the center of \nthe still-collapsing gas cloud like pebbles in a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 492480,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pond, forming a growing cluster that would become \nthe Milky Way’s bulge. As the galactic bulge grew,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 498880,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it was wracked by further waves of supernovae. As \nMoiya mentioned, having excessive exploding stars  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 504560,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in one’s neighborhood can be a problem. Radiation \nmay lead to excessive mutation and degradation of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 510640,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the atmosphere. For example, a supernova within \n150 light years would obliterate our ozone layer.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 517920,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On the other hand, some radiation may be essential \n- because genetic mutation drives evolution.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 524080,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We don’t know where the exact line is in \nterms of getting blasted by nearby supernovae,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 529280,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but the early days of the galactic core were \nsurely above that line. Those supernova waves  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 534640,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have now passed, so you might think \nthat life could take hold on the core.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 538240,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Not so much. The metallicity of that \nregion is now the highest in the galaxy,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 542720,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and high metallicity means too many planets \n- in particular, too many giant planets."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 548480,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We know that having a Jupiter-like \nplanet or two in the outer solar system  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 552720,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can be useful in protecting the \ninner system from infalling comets,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 556560,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but gas giants can also disrupt or destroy \nterrestrial planets. A system with multiple  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 561840,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Jupiter-like planets probably wouldn’t \nstand a chance at forming Earth-analogs."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 566240,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Other factors make the core the worst place in \nthe galaxy. The extreme density of stars will  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 571520,
    "dDurationMs": 15760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have led to frequent close encounters between \nsystems. In our solar system, such encounters  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 587280,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "disturb our Oort cloud, sending comets plummeting \ntowards the inner solar system. A handful of mass  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 593360,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "extinction events from the resulting giant impacts \nwas probably good for driving evolution, but life  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 598560,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "needs time to recover. Overly frequent mass \nextinctions will result in absolute extinction."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 604880,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As the uninhabitable core was forming, pristine \ngas continued to pour into the Galaxy’s growing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 609680,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gravitational field. It was swept up into \na widening whirlpool where it continued to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 614160,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cool and to fragment. Eventually the disk of \ngas converted itself into a disk of stars.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 620480,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It took some time for the emerging spiral disk \nto seed itself with enough heavy elements to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 624960,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "form planetary systems. In fact, some of \nour galaxy still hasn’t had enough time.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 631600,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The outer rim of the Milky Way formed the \nmost recently. It’s still accreting from  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 635920,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the surrounding reservoirs of ancient, Big-Bang \ngas. Towards the rim we see metal-poor gas and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 641680,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "metal-poor stars that they formed - again, not \nthe most likely places to find planets or life."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 648560,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So the inner and outer parts of the Milky Way \ndon’t look promising. But right in between we find  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 653680,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Galactic habitable zone. It emerged around 8 \nbillion years ago, starting out as a band between  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 659680,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "around 20 and 30 thousand light years from the \ncenter, it expanded inwards as the supernova rates  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 666240,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dropped, and outwards as metallicity increased. \nIt now covers around half of the galactic disk."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 672160,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Based on the Milky Way’s formation history, our \nstarting intuition seems right: the Sun and solar  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 677680,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "system are NOT special as far as our galaxy goes. \nBut now that we have so much back-story, perhaps  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 684160,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we can say a little more about the emergence of \nlife-friendly planetary systems. And by “we”,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 689760,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I mean Charlie Lineweaver, Yeshe Fenner and Brad \nGibson from Swinburne University in Australia.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 696240,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This team of astrophysicists estimated the \nhistorical emergence of life-friendly planetary  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 701760,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "systems accounting for all the stuff we talked \nabout. They started with the Milky Way’s history  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 707200,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of star formation and folded in estimates for \nthe probability of the emergence of planets  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 712880,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "based on heavy element abundance; the likelihood \nof surviving obliteration by supernovae;  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 718960,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the probability that life could emerge given \nthe amount of time the system has been around."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 724000,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So what did they find? As we suspected, the Sun \nis not unique, but it’s also not the most typical.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 731760,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Fewer than 10% of stars formed in the Milky Way \nhave optimal conditions for the development of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 737360,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "life, and that would drop down to a percent or two \nif we ruled out the erratic red dwarf stars, the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 744480,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "most common star type. That still leaves billions \nof possible origins for life in the Milky Way,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 752240,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so we haven’t solved the Fermi Paradox. Quite \nthe opposite - we’ve made it worse. This team  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 759200,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "discovered something unexpected: of all the stars \nin the Galaxy that could currently support life,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 765600,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "most of them - 75% - have been around longer \nthan the Sun - by an average of a billion  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 772000,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "years. So if our analysis of the galactic \nhabitable zone was supposed help explain  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 777920,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Fermi Paradox by reducing the potential \norigins for life, it’s done the opposite."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 783200,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One factor that may explain the \napparent absence of technological life  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 787200,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is that other such civilizations just haven’t had \ntime to make their presence known on the galactic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 793360,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "scale. We’ve talked about how it should only take a million years or less for one species  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 798880,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to colonise the galaxy - even if just with robotic \nprobes. But it seems that most earth-analogs have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 806240,
    "dDurationMs": 6917,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a head start of a billion years - more than \nenough time to establish galactic empires.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 813157,
    "dDurationMs": 6443,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So it sounds like we haven’t made progress \nsolving the Fermi Paradox. But actually we have.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 819600,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There is a roadblock in the chain from \nforming a habitable planetary system  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 824640,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to sparking simple life to complexifying into a galactically-visible civilization. We’ve solidly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 831760,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ruled out the first as a so-called “great filter”, \nso now we can think harder about the others.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 838560,
    "dDurationMs": 8400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There is something special about this planetary system, even with our personal bias that we orbit  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 846960,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "most important and yet mundane star in the \napparently uninhabited reaches of space time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 854560,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Do you enjoy mythology from \naround the world? Check out Fate \u0026 Fabled,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 858240,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a new show on PBS's Storied channel. \nCo-hosted by me and Dr. Emily Zarka,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 863520,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we unpack ancient myths and legends to \nfind out why such tales were crafted,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 867600,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and investigate mythology’s influence \non humanity. Be sure to watch our  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 871520,
    "dDurationMs": 5643,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Solar mythology episode, which features a \nvisit from Matt to explain nuclear fusion."
    } ]
  } ]
}
